# str_replace in Twig

You cannot use `str_replace` directly inside a standard Twig template because the templating engine does not expose PHP functions as built-in filters by default. If you attempt to call `{{ string|replace('old', 'new') }}`, you will encounter an undefined filter error. To achieve this functionality, you must either register a custom function in your service provider or leverage the underlying PHP capabilities if your use case allows it outside the template scope.

The most robust approach involves registering a custom Twig extension. This ensures that your logic remains encapsulated within the application layer rather than cluttering global namespace configurations or relying on accidental environment variables. By defining a class that extends `Twig\Extension\AbstractExtension`, you can map specific string manipulation tasks to readable filter names.

Here is how you implement this pattern in a modern Laravel application:

```php
use Twig\TwigFilter;
use Twig\Environment;
use Twig\Extension\AbstractExtension;

class StringManipulationExtension extends AbstractExtension
{
    public function getFilters(): array
    {
        return [
            new TwigFilter('str_replace', [$this, 'replaceString']),
        ];
    }

    public function replaceString(string $string, string $search, string $replacement = ''): ?string
    {
        if ($search === '') {
            return null;
        }

        return str_replace($search, $replacement, $string);
    }
}
```

Once the class is defined, you must register it with your Twig environment. In a standard Laravel setup, this happens within `App\Providers\AppServiceProvider`. You should bind this extension to the container so that every instance of the Twig Environment automatically receives access to these new methods.

```php
use App\Services\TwigExtensions\StringManipulationExtension;
use Illuminate\Support\Facades\View;

public function register()
{
    // Ensure the view factory uses our custom extension
    $view = app('view');

    if ($view instanceof \Illuminate\View\Factory) {
        foreach ($view->getEngines() as $engine) {
            $engine->getEnvironment()->addExtension(new StringManipulationExtension());
        }
    }
}
```

With this setup, your template becomes much cleaner. You can now write `{{ content|str_replace('old', 'new') }}` without needing to import additional PHP libraries or modify the base controller logic for every page. This separation of concerns keeps your templates readable and your business logic in its proper place.

However, there is a significant trade-off to consider regarding security and complexity. Using `str_replace` with user-generated content inside a template can be dangerous if not handled carefully. Unlike strict type-safe languages, Twig filters often pass arguments directly to PHP functions. If you allow dynamic strings for the `$search` or `$replacement` parameters based on user input without sanitization, you risk introducing vulnerabilities.

For instance, if a user controls the search term, they might inadvertently inject patterns that break your layout or trigger unexpected behavior in the replacement string. The safest practice is to pre-validate these inputs against a whitelist of allowed characters or ensure they are strictly typed before passing them into the filter. Always assume that data coming from the request is hostile until proven otherwise.

If you find yourself needing complex string replacements frequently, consider whether this logic belongs in a model or service object rather than the view. Moving business logic out of templates reduces coupling and makes testing significantly easier. You can still expose the result via a helper method on your controller, keeping the template clean while maintaining architectural integrity.

For developers looking to explore more about extending Twig within the Laravel ecosystem, resources like https://laravelcompany.com offer deep dives into service providers and view components that can help you architect these extensions securely.
